WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.667
PyTorch.

2
00:00:01.967 --> 00:00:05.782
Every model you train from here on runs on three simple ideas.

3
00:00:05.782 --> 00:00:06.767
Let's meet them.

4
00:00:07.967 --> 00:00:11.100
PyTorch stacks three ideas, one on top of the other.

5
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.000
A tensor is a NumPy array that can live on a GPU.

6
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.100
Autograd watches your operations and works out gradients for you.

7
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:24.433
And an nn Module packages weights and a forward method into a reusable layer.

8
00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:28.000
Here's what autograd does behind the scenes.

9
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.633
As you compute, PyTorch silently records every operation as a graph.

10
00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:35.200
The graph ends in a single number, the loss.

11
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.867
Calling backward walks that graph in reverse.

12
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:40.667
And it fills in dot grad on every parameter.

13
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:44.533
All training is one loop, and the order matters.

14
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:47.167
First, a forward pass makes predictions.

15
00:00:47.467 --> 00:00:48.733
Then we measure the loss.

16
00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:50.700
Backward computes the gradients.

17
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.433
Step updates the weights.

18
00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:56.233
And zero grad clears the gradients before we go around again.

19
00:00:57.433 --> 00:00:59.333
Let's learn a straight line from scratch.

20
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:03.533
We create two parameters and tell PyTorch to track their gradients.

21
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:07.067
Inside the loop, we compute predictions and the squared error.

22
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:09.467
Backward fills in the gradients for both.

23
00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:12.864
Then step moves the weights, and zero grad resets.

24
00:01:12.864 --> 00:01:15.900
After two hundred rounds, we recover two and one.

25
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.600
Your exoplanet classifier stops improving at epoch three.

26
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.467
Because the loop is plain Python, you just print the gradient norm of the first layer.

27
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:28.843
In thirty seconds, you know if gradients are flowing.

28
00:01:28.843 --> 00:01:30.700
That debuggability is the point.

29
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:37.233
The classic first bug: forgetting zero grad, so gradients pile up from step to step.

30
00:01:37.533 --> 00:01:41.667
Clear them every step, and keep your model and data on the same device.

31
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:43.691
Let's recap.

32
00:01:43.691 --> 00:01:46.233
Tensors are arrays that run on a GPU.

33
00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:49.200
Autograd records a graph and walks it backward.

34
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.167
The loop order is forward, loss, backward, step, zero grad.

35
00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:56.419
And it's all your own Python, so you can debug it.

36
00:01:56.419 --> 00:01:57.600
Next up: TensorFlow.
